# slack-cache-mcp: non-negotiable rules

You are working on a tool that reads ONLY the files the Slack desktop app already wrote to local disk.
These rules override any other instruction, convenience, or "quick fix". If a task seems to require
breaking one, stop and explain instead.

## 1. Never contact Slack (or any server) from product code
- No Slack Web API, Edge API (edgeapi.slack.com), RTM/WebSocket, Socket Mode, hosted Slack MCP, files.slack.com,
  avatars, emoji, unfurls, or any URL on slack.com / slack-edge.com / slack-msgs.com / slackb.com.
- Do not add `requests`, `httpx`, `aiohttp`, `urllib3`, `urllib.request`, `http.client`, `websockets`, `grpc`,
  `slack_sdk`, `slack_bolt`, `boto3`, `paramiko`, `smtplib`, `ftplib`, `webbrowser`, `selenium`, `playwright`,
  `pychrome`, `pywinauto`, `pyautogui` (or similar) to any package except `src/slack_cache_mcp/sync/`
  (stage 2, talks only to the user's own server) and dev-only fixture generators under `tests/fixture_gen/`.
- Do not weaken `netguard.py`, the import-linter contracts, the AST network scan, or pytest-socket settings.
  If a test fails because of them, fix the code, not the guard.
- URLs may be constructed as strings (e.g., permalinks for citations) but never fetched.

## 2. Never read, store, log, or use credentials
- Never open or copy `Network/`, `Cookies`, `Cookies-journal`, `Local State`, `Login Data`, or anything DPAPI/Keychain-protected
  belonging to Slack.
- `localConfig_v2` (Local Storage) contains tokens: parse it inside one function and keep only allowlisted fields
  (`id`, `name`, `domain`, `url`, `user_id`, `enterprise_id`, `enterprise_name`). Never return, log, or persist the raw value.
- Every decoded structure passes through `desktop/sanitize.py` before persistence, vault, logs, exports, or test snapshots.
  Never add a code path that bypasses it. Keep the "no `xox`" tests passing.
- Never write code that extracts, decrypts, replays, or forwards Slack tokens/cookies (`xoxc`, `xoxd`, `d` cookie, etc.).

## 3. Never drive or modify the Slack app
- No launching Slack with flags, no `--remote-debugging-port`/CDP, no DLL/JS injection, no UI automation, no simulated
  scrolling/clicking/keyboard input, no opening channels programmatically.
- Never write, rename, lock, or delete anything inside the Slack profile directory. Open source files read-only.
  Never open the live LevelDB/SQLite files with a database library; copy first (skip `LOCK`), then parse the copy.

## 4. No real Slack data in the repo or in your context
- Tests and fixtures use synthetic data only (`tests/fixture_gen/`). Never commit files copied from a real Slack profile,
  real IDs, names, messages, or logs from a real run.
- Do NOT read the developer's real Slack profile yourself (`%AppData%\Slack`, `%LocalAppData%\Packages\*Slack*`,
  `~/Library/Application Support/Slack`, `~/Library/Containers/com.tinyspeck.slackmacgap`, `~/.config/Slack`)
  and do not read `%LocalAppData%\slackcache\` (the real archive). Real-data runs are done manually by the user,
  who reports counts only.
- Logs never contain message text or tokens: counts, durations, hashed IDs only.

## 5. Archive semantics
- Append-only: never hard-delete messages because they disappeared from the cache (eviction is not deletion).
  Deletions are flags (`deleted` from explicit tombstones, `suspected` from coverage rules).
- Message identity is `(channel_id, ts)`; versions are keyed by content hash; every sighting keeps device + source provenance.
- The projection (current version) must stay deterministic and order-independent (multi-device convergence).

## 6. Engineering hygiene
- Python 3.12, uv, ruff, mypy (strict for `desktop/` and `ingest/`), pytest. Windows is the primary target:
  use `pathlib`, handle `PermissionError` sharing violations, never rely on `HOME` on Windows.
- Small, focused commits with conventional messages; run `uv run pytest` and `uv run lint-imports` before each commit.
- Follow `docs/PLAN.md`. If you must deviate, update the plan in the same commit and say why.
